-\section{A minimal model}
-\label{sec:minimal}
-
-\subsection{Model}
-$n=500$ agents sit on an Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi graph with mean degree 10; opinions start uniform in $[0,1]^d$ (the \emph{unit} space) or $[-1,1]^d$ (the \emph{centred} space), for $d \in \{2,4,8\}$.
-Each round draws a random matching of the graph's edges, so no agent takes part in two conversations in a round.
-For every matched pair within $\varepsilon$, both agents move a fraction $\mu=0.3$ toward the other's previous opinion under the metric's update rule \citep{deffuant2000}, and are clipped back into the cube.
-Runs last 2{,}000 rounds.
-A seed fixes the graph, the initial opinions and the sequence of matchings, so runs with the same seed differ only in the gate and the update rule.
-The model is described in ODD format \citep{grimm2020} in Appendix~\ref{app:odd}.
+\section{Choice 2: when coverage is matched}
+\label{sec:matching}
 
-\subsection{Threshold rules}
-In each cell (space, dimension, reference coverage) Euclidean distance runs at the $\varepsilon$ that admits a share $c \in \{5\%, 15\%, 30\%\}$ of pairs at the start.
-Cosine and angular distance each run under three rules:
+\subsection{Design}
+We use a minimal Deffuant model (Appendix~\ref{app:odd}): 500 agents on an Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi graph, random matchings each round, $\mu = 0.3$.
+The factorial crosses the opinion space (unit or centred), the dimension ($d \in \{2,4,8\}$) and a reference coverage $c \in \{5\%, 15\%, 30\%\}$: 18 cells.
+In each cell Euclidean distance runs at the $\varepsilon$ that admits a share $c$ of pairs at the start, and cosine and angular distance each run under three threshold rules:
 \begin{description}
-\item[raw:] the same number as the Euclidean $\varepsilon$;
-\item[matched at $t=0$:] the $\varepsilon$ that admits the same share $c$ of pairs at the start;
-\item[matched over the run:] the $\varepsilon$ whose pass rate, averaged over the whole run, equals the Euclidean run's (found by bisection on ten pilot seeds that are not reused).
+\item[shared:] the same number as Euclidean's $\varepsilon$;
+\item[matched at the start:] the $\varepsilon$ that admits the same share $c$ of pairs at $t = 0$;
+\item[matched over the run:] the $\varepsilon$ whose pass rate over the first 2{,}000 rounds equals Euclidean's, found by bisection on 50 pilot seeds that are not reused. All \VCalTotal\ calibrations met their tolerance of 0.005 (largest error \VCalMaxErr), and the pass rate rose with $\varepsilon$ at every step (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:calibration}).
 \end{description}
-Because initial opinions are independent of the graph, the share of random pairs and the share of neighbour pairs within $\varepsilon$ coincide at $t=0$.
-
-\subsection{Outcomes}
-Final opinion variance (mean over dimensions); directional dispersion, $1 - \lVert \overline{x/\lVert x \rVert} \rVert$; the number of opinion groups holding at least 1\% of agents, where a group is a connected set of final opinions within $0.1\varepsilon$ of each other under the run's metric; the effective number of groups, $1/\sum_k s_k^2$ over group shares $s_k$; and the realised pass rate, the share of matched pairs within $\varepsilon$ over the whole run.
 Each cell has 100 seeds, paired across metrics and rules.
+Euclidean dynamics are unchanged by shifting and rescaling the cube, so its 18 cells are 9 distinct designs, each run in both coordinate systems.
+Statistics were fixed before the runs were analysed.
+Gaps to the Euclidean run are differences for the pass rate and $\log_2$ ratios for everything else, with paired bootstrap 95\% intervals.
+Summaries across cases are medians of absolute gaps; the signed gaps are in the result tables.
+We call a gap \emph{equivalent to zero} when its 90\% interval lies within $\pm 0.02$ for the pass rate or $\pm 0.25$ on the $\log_2$ scale, a two-one-sided-tests criterion \citep{lakens2017}.
+The primary outcome is the number of opinion groups holding at least 1\% of agents, counted with one ruler for every metric (Euclidean distance in centred coordinates, radius 5\% of the cube's diagonal) after 2{,}000 rounds.
+Cosine and angular results are reported separately; there are \VCases\ metric--cell pairs per rule.
 
