+\section{An audit list for comparisons of distance measures}
 \label{sec:protocol}
 
 \begin{framed}
-\noindent\textbf{Comparing distance measures in a bounded-confidence model}
+\noindent\textbf{Before reporting that a distance measure changes a result}
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{Centre the space, or say why not, and check every step that depends on the origin.} Angular distances measure direction from the origin; put the origin at neutral. The same goes for any multiplicative step (shrinkage by trust, decay, scaling): it pulls toward the origin, so the origin must mean what the model intends.
-\item \textbf{Never share a numerical bound.} A shared $\varepsilon$ is not a control: it admits different shares of pairs under each metric. Start from bounds that admit the same share of \emph{network-neighbour} pairs, and report that share.
-\item \textbf{Match on realised coverage, and report it.} Coverage matched at the start drifts apart during a run. Report the share of attempted interactions that passed, by metric, and calibrate each bound so that this realised share matches (bisection on pilot seeds that are not reused).
-\item \textbf{Check for monotone equivalence.} If one distance is a strictly increasing function of another, matched gates admit the same pairs, and any difference comes from the update rule.
-\item \textbf{Test the comparison directly.} A claim that a metric changes an effect is a claim about an interaction; test it, not each cell against zero.
+\item \textbf{Audit the origin.} Recode the opinion space by an affine shift and rerun a few seeds; any outcome that changes depends on the origin. Centre the space at a meaningful neutral point, or say why not, and treat every shrinkage or decay target (trust, attention, forgetting) as an explicit, varied assumption.
+\item \textbf{Do not rely on a shared threshold value.} A shared $\varepsilon$ admits a different share of pairs under each metric. If one is used, show that realised coverage matches.
+\item \textbf{Report the response to $\varepsilon$, not one matched point.} Plot outcomes against \emph{realised} coverage across a range of thresholds. A point matched at the start and a point matched over the run answer different questions (the effect including, or excluding, the metric's effect on how often agents interact); name the one you report.
+\item \textbf{Check for monotone equivalence.} Metrics related by a strictly increasing function are one gate once matched (Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone}); differences between them come from the update rule or the measurement.
+\item \textbf{Test the comparison.} A claim that a metric changes an effect is an interaction; test it directly \citep{gelman2006}, and support ``no difference'' with an equivalence test \citep{lakens2017}, not a non-significant one.
+\item \textbf{Report the ruler and the stopping time.} Count groups with one rule for every metric, report the share of runs that have not settled, and show that conclusions survive a longer run.
 \end{enumerate}
 \end{framed}
 
 \section{Limitations}
-Opinions start uniform and independent; correlated or clustered starting opinions change how coverage depends on the metric, though not the argument for matching it.
-We study pairwise Deffuant updates only; Hegselmann--Krause averaging over all close neighbours is a natural next case.
-Minimal-model outcomes are measured after a fixed 2{,}000 rounds; at the lowest coverage some runs have not settled by then, so their group counts describe a transient state (the comparison is still paired at the same horizon).
-The case study rests on one model; the trust mechanism we identify is specific to it, though the general point (multiplicative steps inherit the meaning of the origin) is not.
-Matching on coverage is one notion of a fair comparison among several: one could instead match the expected number of interactions per agent or the time to convergence.
-Our point is that some such matching is needed and should be reported, not that coverage is the only right choice.
+Opinions start uniform and independent; clustered or correlated starting opinions change how coverage depends on the metric, though not the argument for reporting it.
+We study pairwise Deffuant updates only; for Hegselmann--Krause averaging the natural analogue of coverage is the mean number of neighbours inside the confidence region, which we did not test.
+The case study rests on one model; its trust mechanism is specific to it, while the general point (multiplicative steps inherit the meaning of the origin) is not.
+Our list of hidden choices is not exhaustive: the network, the update order and the size of the population are choices of the same kind.
 
